{=============================================================================
  <Title>
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0336 - Blasius flat-plate boundary layer
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Solution of the classic Blasius equation of the laminar boundary layer over
  a flat plate, as in the original EES example: the third-order ODE
  w``` + 0.5*w*w`` = 0 is integrated with the Integral function from eta = 0
  to eta = 5, with the initial conditions w(0) = 0, w'(0) = 0 and w''(0) =
  0.33206 (the shooting parameter: w'(eta) -> 1 for large eta is obtained for
  w''(0) = 0.33206). The dimensionless velocity w'(eta) is then mapped to the
  dimensional coordinates x and y for air at 25 °C.

  Inputs : w''(0) = 0.33206, eta_end = 5, step = 0.1, Re = 1e5, x = 0.5 m,
           T_air = 25 °C
  Outputs: w(eta), w'(eta), w''(eta) at eta_end; u, y, u_inf, mu, rho, nu
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ~/Nextcloud/thermo_models/EES_library/Blasius.EES (EES X8.805),
           (c) F-Chart Software / S. A. Klein, MIT via EES-Community/ees-examples
  Status : blocked - CoolSolve does not accept the EES prime notation in a
           variable name (CS-GAP-NAME-PRIME); the runnable variant
           blasius_flat_plate_coolsolve.eescode renames the five derivatives.
=============================================================================}

"!Solution to Blasius problem - laminar flow over a flat plate."

"The boundary layer flow over a flat plate can be reduced to the solution of the following nonlinear ordinary differential equation.  Three different solutions are presented here: use of the parametric table, use of a fixed step integration and use of the EES automatic step size.  "

 w``` + 0.5* w *w`` = 0

"The initial conditions are:"
w_0=0
w`_0=0
w``_0=0.33206  "This is a so called `shooting problem`, you need to find the value of w``_0 by trial and error so that w`=1 at large eta. Use Newton's method."

$if ParametricTable
"This set uses the Parametric table.  With a step of 0.01 the solution is exact to the number of digits in the table."
w=w_0+Integral(w`, eta)
w`=w`_0+Integral(w``,eta)
w``=w``_0+Integral(w```,eta)
$elseIf

$integralTable eta:1  w, w`, w``, w```
"This set uses the fixed step integration - gives exactly the same answer as Parametric table method when step=parametric table step but also does very well with step=0.1."
w=w_0+Integral(w`, eta, start, stop, step)
w`=w`_0+Integral(w``,eta,start, stop, step)
w``=w``_0+Integral(w```,eta,start, stop, step)

"This set uses the automatic step size routine in EES.  "
{w=w_0+Integral(w`, eta, start, stop)
w`=w`_0+Integral(w``,eta,start, stop)
w``=w``_0+Integral(w```,eta,start, stop)}
$endif

Start=0
Stop=5 "it is traditional to use eta=5 as the top of the boundary layer."
step=0.1


"Integral solution"
u\u_infinity=1.5*(eta/5)-0.5*(eta/5)^3

"Dimensional quantities"
Re=1e5
Re=(u_infinity*x)/nu
x=0.5
T_air=25
mu=viscosity(air, T=T_air)
rho=density(air, T=T_air, P=101300)
nu=mu/rho
eta=y/sqrt((x*nu)/u_infinity)
u/u_infinity=w`
